<resources>
    <!-- Default for Settings.Global.DEVICE_NAME, $1 = MODEL.
         Stock reports the marketing name rather than the model code; the model
         code TB390FU is what ro.product.model already carries. -->
    <string name="def_device_name_simple">Idea Tab Pro Gen 2</string>

    <!-- Default for Settings.System.USER_ROTATION. AOSP ships 0
         (packages/SettingsProvider/res/values/defaults.xml:118).

         This is the second half of "default to landscape"; the first half is
         ro.bootanim.set_orientation_logical_0 in properties/system.prop. Both
         are needed, and neither is sufficient alone:

           - The sysprop sets DisplayRotation.mRotation at construction
             (DisplayRotation.java:271-272 via readDefaultDisplayRotation),
             which is what the boot animation and the very first frames use.
           - But auto-rotate ships OFF (def_accelerometer_rotation is false,
             defaults.xml:33), and with it off DisplayRotation takes the
             USER_ROTATION_LOCKED branch (:1219-1233) and returns mUserRotation.
             That is loaded from def_user_rotation by
             DatabaseHelper.java:802-803 on first boot, so a 0 there overwrites
             the sysprop's 90 the moment the settings database is read.

         1 = Surface.ROTATION_90, which with the panel's install orientation of
         270 composes to identity into the 3504x2190 framebuffer
         (DisplayDevice.cpp:242 adds the WM rotation and the physical
         orientation), i.e. the natural landscape scanout.

         This covers Setup Wizard and the lock screen too: both end up on the
         same DisplayRotation path. Turning auto-rotate on later makes the
         sensor take over, as usual — this only decides where the device starts
         and where it returns to when rotation is locked. -->
    <integer name="def_user_rotation">1</integer>
</resources>
